It is tempting to try to evaluate the contribution of each phenomenon and somehow sum them. However, at the beginning of alloying, the analyzed materials present large grains, in such a way that it is not possible to accurately estimate the average grain size, since there are few grains in each sample, so it is not possible to evaluate the HPE influence on the Vickers microhardness. On the other hand, at the end of alloying, it is not possible to measure the microhardness of a single grain, since the Vickers indentation covers multiple grains, even after load reduction. Therefore, it is difficult to isolate both contributions for hardening. To overcome this drawback, it is desirable to define a hardness increment function, which depends on the alloy composition. Thus: 4 VMH q = VMH t + I h x \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
