				\begin{document}$$U_{{\text{t}}} = U_{{{\text{SS}}}} + U_{{{\text{TM}}}}$$\end{document} where  U t ,  U SS , and  U TM  are the total deformation energy, the deformation energy caused by the formation of solid solution, and the deformation energy caused by the martensitic transformation, respectively. Therefore, if an alloying element deforms the crystal lattice to a high degree, it introduces an “additional step” for the martensitic transformation to occur, since it increases the value of U SS . In practical terms, this means that the crystal lattice must undergo a more severe thermal compression, that is, a greater decrease in its volume during cooling for the phase to form. Such compression occurs by lowering the temperature of the crystal lattice to a temperature below that required to transform into “unalloyed austenite.” The temperature for the start of the martensitic transformation (the  M s  temperature) is reduced after the addition of more Sn because, as the phase transformation is very fast, occurring at a rate of the order of the rate of sound [ 15 ], it is not possible to isolate the two phenomena (the compensation of the deformation caused by the alloying element and the martensitic transformation itself).

It can be seen in the DSC curves of Fig.  9  that the 10Mn-xSn ( x  = 0.5; 1; 2) alloys always have a  M s  temperature higher than  A s .  Alloys with the TMT can be classified according to their mechanical behavior, which can be described by the difference between these temperatures. It is known that [ 61 ]: 7 Δ T M s - A s = Δ G el - 2 Δ G dis \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
