				\begin{document}$$\Delta T_{Ms - As} = \Delta G_{{{\text{el}}}} - 2\Delta G_{{{\text{dis}}}}$$\end{document} where Δ T Ms-As  is the difference between the  M s  and  A s  temperatures, Δ G el  is the stored elastic energy resulting from the TMT, and Δ G dis  is the energy dissipated during this transformation. When  M s  <  A s  (ΔT Ms-As  < 0), the alloy dissipates more energy than it stores, being type I. When  M s  >  A s  (ΔT Ms-As  > 0), the alloy stores more elastic energy than it dissipates, having type II behavior [ 61 ]. Therefore, the 10Mn-xSn ( x  = 0.5; 1; 2) alloys are type II. Aging, however, decreases the intensity of this behavior, since it decreases the value of ΔT Ms-As .
